nts fell 9% in Q2, the steepest drop among large makers (n3.d3).\n- Voltaris's share of Caldrian shipments fell from 31% to 27%. Tarnwell and imports gained (n3.d4).\n- Quarterly revenue was C$905M in 2025Q3, C$960M in 2025Q4, C$980M in 2026Q1 and C$890M in 2026Q2 (n3.d5). The last 4 quarters sum to C$3.735B.\n- Shipments trail orders by about two quarters (n3.d3). The Q2 decline therefore probably reflects weak orders in late 2025 and early 2026. Weakness could persist into 2027.\n\n**Implication**\n- Hitting C$4.2B needs about 12% growth over the trailing-4-quarter run rate of C$3.735B, which is about C$1.05B per quarter. The latest quarter was C$890M, so that would need about 18% above it. This comes while the industry is turning down and Voltaris is losing share.\n- If the 27% share holds and the industry is flat to -4%, 2027 revenue would be about C$3.4–3.6B. That assumes Q2's C$890M quarterly pace (about C$3.56B annualised), adjusted for the industry change. Share erosion toward 25% would take it lower, to about C$3.3B.\n- Reaching C$4.2B would need an industry rebound of roughly +10% or more, plus share recovery or price increases. Nothing in the industry data supports that.\n\n**Caveats**\n- Revenue may include non-transformer lines, and the data is only through Q2 2026.\n- The share figures are from a single quarter.\n- I did not look at Voltaris's orders or backlog. Those are n2's task and could offset this view.","ttft_ms":2920,"type":"result","duration_ms":23711,"uuid":"a5d55778-7fdb-4455-91b9-31d2c60292ef","ttft_stream_ms":1940,"time_to_request_ms":92,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":13,"system_prompt":8,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":12,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":6,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":11,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build"